// EXPECT-FAIL: codegen
// ⚠️ DEBT: rejected cleanly, but only at CODEGEN and with NO source span — the user
//    gets a name, not a file:line. Should become `typecheck`. F1 stage 7 / F2 stage 5.
//    (Was `compiler-crash`: an unhandled Zig error printed a compiler backtrace.
//     Fixed 2026-07-17 via main.zig's userErrorHint. Plan P0-5.)
// NEGATIVE test: must FAIL to compile (specs §10 #6/#7, design/F1 §2.6).
//
// `t.describe()` where Thing has NO `describe` method, and a global `describe`
// exists. Must be rejected, not silently resolved to the global.
//
// It used to compile and call the wrong function. The call path's bare
// `func_map.get(fa.field)` fallback (llvm_codegen.zig:1236) resolved a VARIABLE's
// member against the global function table — the sibling of the value-path bug
// that put a function address into a `string` field and cost months as "string
// heap corruption" (§10 #6). The value path was guarded then; this one was not.
//
// The ZERO-ARG global is the point. `t.describe()` lowers to `call @describe()`
// with 0 args, so the arity MATCHES and the LLVM verifier — which caught the
// mismatched-arity version loudly — is completely blind. Measured before the fix:
//
//     got=WRONG-global-fn      exit 0
//
// A clean compile, a wrong answer, no diagnostic anywhere.
import assert;

fn describe(): string { return "WRONG-global-fn"; }

struct Thing {
    pub v: int,
    init(v: int) { self.v = v; }
    // deliberately NO `describe` method
}

@test
fn t_method_must_not_resolve_to_global(): void {
    let t = Thing(7);
    assert.equalStr(t.describe(), "anything");   // must not compile
}
